WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.222 line:88% align:center
Checkpoint drugs work by releasing the brakes on a T cell — but they help only a minority of patients, because only a handful of those brakes are drugged.

2
00:00:10.222 --> 00:00:13.093 line:88% align:center
So we went looking for the rest, genome-wide.

3
00:00:13.093 --> 00:00:16.027 line:88% align:center
This is Brakepoint, built with Claude Science.

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:21.944 line:88% align:center
Those brakes matter beyond checkpoint therapy — they also throttle engineered CAR-T.

5
00:00:21.944 --> 00:00:29.593 line:88% align:center
So across the genome, which genes are the brakes — which knockdowns push a human T cell toward a stronger effector state?

6
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:39.498 line:88% align:center
6 million primary human CD4 T cells, from the Gladstone Institutes.

7
00:00:39.498 --> 00:00:44.493 line:88% align:center
Two donors, out of an intended four.

8
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.006 line:88% align:center
How do you find the brakes in twelve thousand knockdowns?

9
00:00:49.006 --> 00:00:57.981 line:88% align:center
The reflex is to rank by significance — but at two million cells that breaks down: over 97% of the tested knockdowns clear the bar.

10
00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:00.791 line:88% align:center
So we rank by causal effect size instead.

11
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.422 line:88% align:center
But effect size alone still points at the wrong genes — the biggest hits are the cell's own signaling machinery.

12
00:01:08.422 --> 00:01:14.440 line:88% align:center
So we add what a magnitude ranking leaves out: direction — toward the effector program, or away.

13
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.829 line:88% align:center
Now the machinery falls away, and the candidate brakes rise into view.

14
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:21.475 line:88% align:center
And this isn't a static picture.

15
00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:31.109 line:88% align:center
Every one of the eleven thousand tested knockdowns is here to explore — search any gene, hover any point, and read its causal effect and its direction.

16
00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:33.151 line:88% align:center
It's the real leaderboard, live.

17
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.042 line:88% align:center
And that's the edge.

18
00:01:35.042 --> 00:01:38.997 line:88% align:center
Differential expression finds correlations — not what to drug.

19
00:01:38.997 --> 00:01:41.994 line:88% align:center
Genetics points to a locus, rarely a direction.

20
00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:49.329 line:88% align:center
We measure what a knockdown actually does, and which way it pushes — then weigh it against genetics and the clinic.

21
00:01:49.933 --> 00:02:04.611 line:88% align:center
From that map, five prior-informed candidates: CBLB, CD5, DGKA, SMAD3, and UBASH3A — each scored across seven lines of evidence, from causal effect and direction to human genetics and clinical precedent.

22
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:06.390 line:88% align:center
Our lead is CBLB.

23
00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:13.060 line:88% align:center
It's a natural off-switch for T-cell activation, and inhibitors are already in early-phase trials.

24
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.689 line:88% align:center
Its genetics point the same way, and it lands in our brake quadrant.

25
00:02:17.689 --> 00:02:21.500 line:88% align:center
CD5 and DGKA come next — and both hold up across donors.

26
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.456 line:88% align:center
And we're honest about what two donors can support.

27
00:02:25.456 --> 00:02:29.666 line:88% align:center
CD5 and DGKA hold up in both; the other three ride on one donor.

28
00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:39.667 line:88% align:center
As a group, known brakes aren't significantly enriched — so this is a ranked shortlist for the full cohort, a hypothesis to test, not a finished answer.

29
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:48.822 line:88% align:center
Five candidate brakes, led by CBLB — every number reproducible from one command, and checked against a real bug we caught ourselves.

30
00:02:48.822 --> 00:02:54.504 line:88% align:center
Explore the live map, clone the code, and help find the brakes today's drugs still miss.

31
00:02:54.504 --> 00:02:56.183 line:88% align:center
Built with Claude Science.
